                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The Mind of Marlo is a strange little game, but that is also what makes it interesting. It is a short point-and-click adventure that follows Marlo, a normal guy who suddenly starts going through some very unusual changes. The game presents itself almost like a documentary, which gives it a different style compared to most indie games.\n\nThe biggest thing this game has going for it is the personality. The humour is weird, but it has a lot of charm. The way the story is told makes the silly situations feel even funnier because everything is treated so seriously. It feels like watching a strange comedy show where nobody questions how ridiculous things are. The writing is probably the main reason to play this game.\n\nThe gameplay is simple. You explore areas, click on objects, solve small puzzles, and move the story forward. The puzzles are not very difficult, but they fit the type of game it is. They are there to keep you involved rather than being the main challenge.\n\nThe presentation is also pretty good for a small indie game. The art style matches the strange comedy, and the overall design gives it a lot of character. It feels like a game made with a clear idea instead of something trying to copy bigger titles.\n\nThe main problem is the length. You can finish the game fairly quickly, and once you complete it there is not much reason to come back. The gameplay is also quite basic, so if you are not interested in the story or humour, there is not much else keeping you playing.\n\nAnother thing is that the comedy will not work for everyone. The jokes are very specific and sometimes a little random. Some people will find it funny and memorable, while others might just find it strange and not really connect with it.\n\nOverall, The Mind of Marlo is a fun short adventure that succeeds because of its creativity and humour. It is not a deep game and it does not have much replay value, but it has more personality than many small indie projects. Since it is free, it is easy to recommend giving it a try if you like unusual story games.\n\nRating: 7/10",
